  <Vertical id="wj1_intro">
    <Markdown>
Hello! In this activity you will interact with fictional characters in a scenario created to help you
think about your writing process. There will be a chat on the left side of the screen that you can advance
with the 'continue' button. Sometimes activities, prompts, and text will appear here on the right side.
When this happens, please read and interact with it before advancing the chat.
    </Markdown>
    <Done id="wj1_intro_done" label="Next" />
  </Vertical>

  <Markdown id="wj1_welcome">
## Writing Journal 1: Who Am I As a Writer?

It's the first week of your First-Year Composition course, and **Professor Ellington** has just assigned
your first Writing Journal:

> **Who am I as a writer?**
>
> Start noticing the writing you already do — not only papers and assignments, but *all* of it — and
> think about what it says about you as a writer.

Here's the thing: you've never really thought of yourself as a writer. You write constantly — messages,
notes, posts, replies — but almost none of it feels like the kind of writing the assignment means. You're
not sure any of it counts.

So when **Alma**, who took 1010 last year, texts you to come to the **Writers' Circle** — a few students
who meet on the library's second floor to talk about what they're writing and what's tripping them up —
you go. It's Thursday, 4:02 pm. Lee and Richard are already mid-conversation.

**By the end of this journal you will have:**

- built a **Writer Board** — a map of the kinds of writing you actually do, for school and outside it,
  and how often;
- worked out **why** you do different kinds of writing — the part a list of *what* can't tell you;
- read through the Circle's eight **writer types** and **chosen the one** that sounds most like you — or
  written your own; and
- told a story from your writing life that shows **why that type fits you — or where it doesn't quite.**

There are no wrong answers here and no grade for being one kind of writer or another. Work through the conversation on the left — each step opens an activity here on the right.
  </Markdown>

