  <!-- ===================================================================
       TASK 3 — YOUR WRITER TYPE  (STUDENT-SELECTED; NO LLM, NO ASSIGNMENT)
       The culminating screen puts the Circle's whole card deck in front of the
       student and lets them choose. Three pieces:

         1. wj1_type_cards — the eight authored cards, parked in <Hidden> so they
            live in the OLX DOM as addressable blocks without rendering twice.
            Each card is a Vertical with a `title` (the Carousel prints it in its
            own header), the card table, and a "This one's me" ActionButton.
         2. wj1_type_carousel — a <Carousel> over those eight ids. It shows ONE
            card at a time with prev/next arrows; wrap="true" so the deck is a
            loop rather than a dead end at either edge.
         3. wj1_type_result — the confirmation. One IntakeGate per type on
            @wj1_type_choice.value, so the chosen card is MOUNTED (not merely
            visible) and therefore survives into the printed PDF, which is a
            photograph of the live DOM. See the wj1_print comment below.

       The Carousel's own `value` field tracks whichever card is on screen right
       now — it changes every time an arrow is clicked, so it is a CURSOR, not a
       choice. The student's actual selection is a separate, deliberate write:
       each card's button fires SetFieldAction into wj1_type_choice (a Hidden
       LineInput) with an explicit semantic code. Browsing on afterwards cannot
       silently change what they picked; clicking a different card's button can,
       which is what changing your mind should look like.

       Card id -> stored code (kept 1:1 and semantic for the event stream):
         wj1_card_community_writer -> community_writer   🤝
         wj1_card_explorer         -> explorer           🧭
         wj1_card_technician       -> technician         🔧
         wj1_card_hobbyist         -> hobbyist           🌿
         wj1_card_dreamer          -> dreamer            💭
         wj1_card_practitioner     -> practitioner       🔁
         wj1_card_finisher         -> finisher           🏁
         wj1_card_pragmatist       -> pragmatist         🎯
         wj1_card_custom           -> custom             ✍️  (student-written)
       (wj1_card_cover is card 0 and stores nothing — it is not a choice.)

       ORDER — why a cover card and not a shuffle. Whichever card comes first in
       a deck gets picked more, so the preference was to randomize the eight
       typed cards and PIN the write-your-own card last. Carousel can't do that:
       `randomize` (Carousel.ts:36) shuffles the entire item list with no pin or
       exclude, and when randomize is off _Carousel.tsx:49-52 rewrites the stored
       `order` field back to the authored sequence on every render — so there is
       no author- or content-side way to hold one item in place, and the platform
       is not ours to change here. A whole-deck shuffle would drop "write your
       own" into the middle, where it reads as confusion rather than as the
       deck's last word.

       So: authored order behind a NEUTRAL COVER CARD. Card 0 has no "This one's
       me" button, so the first card a student sees is not a choice at all, which
       is what the shuffle was for. The eight then run in the same order as the
       orientations table and the Writer Types tab, so a student meets them
       identically everywhere, and the blank card is last — "and if none of those
       is you…", rather than an escape hatch offered before they've looked.

       The custom card's two inputs are not required: the button sets `custom`
       regardless, because a student who has decided none of the eight fits has
       already made the choice this screen asks for. Discourage, don't block.

       HISTORY (Aug 2026): this screen used to ASSIGN a type — the two biggest
       reasons (wj1_why_top2) revealed one or two cards behind a "Generate your
       writer profile" button. The TA reported that landing on a personality-quiz
       result felt limiting, so the assignment machinery (wj1_generate_btn,
       wj1_generate_flag, the reason->card IntakeGates, and the "does this feel
       like you" question wj1_fit) is gone. The two biggest reasons keep their
       role as reflection back in Why I Write; they no longer gate anything here.
       =================================================================== -->

  <Vertical id="wj1_profile">
    <Markdown>
## Your Writer Type

You built your **Writer Board** and worked out **why** you do different kinds of writing. Now here's the
Circle's whole deck — **eight writer types**, the same shorthand Alma, Lee, and Richard use, plus a **blank
ninth card** for writing your own if none of the eight is you.

Read through them with the arrows. Your two biggest reasons may **point toward** one of these, but nothing
here is deciding for you: **you pick.** When you find the one that sounds most like you, click **This one's
me** on that card. You can keep browsing afterwards and change it if a later card fits better.

Remember Alma's rule: it's a **shorthand, not a verdict.** You know things about yourself no card does.
    </Markdown>

    <!-- The deck. Parked in <Hidden> so these eight blocks exist in the OLX DOM
         and are addressable by id, but render only where the Carousel puts them
         (Hidden renders its kids into the tree and returns null). Card text is
         unchanged from the assigned-profile version except that the old "Why
         you're seeing it" row is gone — nothing is being shown BECAUSE of
         anything now. Each card's inner Markdown carries its own id so the
         confirmation section below can <Use> the table without the button. -->
    <Hidden id="wj1_type_cards">

      <!-- Card 0: the cover. It carries no "This one's me" button, so the first
           thing the student lands on is not a choice — which is the whole job it
           does. See the ORDER note in the comment above this screen. -->
      <Vertical id="wj1_card_cover" title="🃏 The Circle's Deck">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_cover_text">
| 🃏 The Circle's Deck | Nine cards. Not one of them is yours yet. |
| :-- | :-- |
| **How to read it** | Arrows move you through. Read **all** of them before you take one — Richard's standing complaint about this deck is that people stop at the first card that flatters them. |
| **If none of them fit** | The last card is blank, and it's for writing your own. |
        </Markdown>
      </Vertical>

      <Vertical id="wj1_card_community_writer" title="🤝 The Community Writer">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_community_writer_text">
| 🤝 The Community Writer | Core motivation: connection |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to reach other people — messages, group chats, replies, shared docs — and you get sharper through back-and-forth with real readers. |
| **A growth edge** | Take in feedback without becoming dependent on it. |
        </Markdown>
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_community_writer" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_community_writer_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="community_writer" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

      <Vertical id="wj1_card_explorer" title="🧭 The Explorer">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_explorer_text">
| 🧭 The Explorer | Core motivation: curiosity |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to find out what you think — often not knowing where a piece is going until you're in it. Writing is a way of thinking, and you like to experiment. *(Lee's type.)* |
| **A growth edge** | Decide when exploring is the goal and when a piece needs to land somewhere. |
        </Markdown>
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_explorer" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_explorer_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="explorer" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

      <Vertical id="wj1_card_technician" title="🔧 The Technician">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_technician_text">
| 🔧 The Technician | Core motivation: getting it done, and right |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to accomplish a task cleanly — clear, organized, no wasted motion — and you like doing it properly the first time. *(Richard's type.)* |
| **A growth edge** | The parts of writing beyond correctness — voice, surprise, the reader's experience. |
        </Markdown>
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_technician" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_technician_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="technician" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

      <Vertical id="wj1_card_hobbyist" title="🌿 The Hobbyist">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_hobbyist_text">
| 🌿 The Hobbyist | Core motivation: personal fulfillment |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write because you *like* it. You don't necessarily need a big audience or a grade — the writing itself is the point, seen or not. |
| **A growth edge** | Protect the joy of it even as you push to grow. |
        </Markdown>
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_hobbyist" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_hobbyist_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="hobbyist" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

      <Vertical id="wj1_card_dreamer" title="💭 The Dreamer">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_dreamer_text">
| 💭 The Dreamer | Core motivation: expression |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to get what's inside into words — to work out how you feel and make sense of things. There's an inner life your writing reaches for, polished or not. |
| **A growth edge** | Turn some of that inner material into pieces you finish and share. |
        </Markdown>
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_dreamer" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_dreamer_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="dreamer" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

      <Vertical id="wj1_card_practitioner" title="🔁 The Practitioner">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_practitioner_text">
| 🔁 The Practitioner | Core motivation: steady practice |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write regularly and usefully — to keep track, to remember, to stay on top of things. You trust the habit more than inspiration, and it adds up. |
| **A growth edge** | Let some of that steady practice become writing you do for its own sake. |
        </Markdown>
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_practitioner" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_practitioner_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="practitioner" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

      <Vertical id="wj1_card_finisher" title="🏁 The Finisher">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_finisher_text">
| 🏁 The Finisher | Core motivation: finishing something real |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to end up with a finished thing — something you can point to and say *I made that.* You have, or are building, the persistence to see a piece through. |
| **A growth edge** | Treat finishing as the start of a writing life, not a one-time test. |
        </Markdown>
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_finisher" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_finisher_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="finisher" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

      <Vertical id="wj1_card_pragmatist" title="🎯 The Pragmatist">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_pragmatist_text">
| 🎯 The Pragmatist | Core motivation: audience &amp; impact |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to *reach* people — to make a point, change a mind, get a result. You think about the reader on the other end, not just the words on the page. |
| **A growth edge** | Keep your own voice and purpose alongside the goal of landing with an audience. |
        </Markdown>
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_pragmatist" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_pragmatist_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="pragmatist" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

      <!-- The ninth card: a blank one. Eight is eight, and a student who doesn't
           fit any of them shouldn't have to pick the least wrong. This card is
           the only one whose content comes from the student, so it can't be
           <Use>d into the confirmation the way the others are — the confirmation
           re-renders it from wj1_type_custom_name / wj1_type_custom_text below.

           Neither input gates the choice: the button sets `custom` whether or
           not they've written anything. Discourage, don't block. -->
      <Vertical id="wj1_card_custom" title="✍️ Write your own">
        <Markdown id="wj1_card_custom_prompt">
| ✍️ Write your own | Core motivation: yours to name |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | None of the eight? Then the deck is short a card, and the Circle's rule is that you make it. This shorthand was invented by students sitting where you're sitting — it's yours to extend. |
| **A growth edge** | Naming your own is harder than taking one that's already written. It's also worth more. |

**Name it.** *(Alma's is "the community writer." Yours can be anything you'd actually say out loud.)*
        </Markdown>
        <LineInput id="wj1_type_custom_name" placeholder="The ___ Writer" />
        <Markdown>**Now say what it looks like** — a sentence or two. What do you write, and what is it for?</Markdown>
        <TextArea id="wj1_type_custom_text" rows="4" placeholder="I write mostly to..." />
        <ActionButton id="wj1_pick_custom" label="This one's me">
          <SetFieldAction id="wj1_pick_custom_set" target="wj1_type_choice" field="value" value="custom" />
        </ActionButton>
      </Vertical>

    </Hidden>

    <!-- The deck itself. wrap="true" so the eight cards are a loop: a student who
         arrives undecided can go either direction and never hit a dead arrow. -->
    <Carousel id="wj1_type_carousel" wrap="true">
      wj1_card_cover,
      wj1_card_community_writer, wj1_card_explorer, wj1_card_technician, wj1_card_hobbyist,
      wj1_card_dreamer, wj1_card_practitioner, wj1_card_finisher, wj1_card_pragmatist,
      wj1_card_custom
    </Carousel>

    <!-- The choice. A Hidden LineInput rather than the Carousel's own value,
         which is a browsing cursor and moves with the arrows. -->
    <Hidden id="wj1_type_choice_holder">
      <LineInput id="wj1_type_choice" />
    </Hidden>

    <!-- The confirmation. The outer gate keeps the screen quiet until they've
         chosen; the eight inner gates mount exactly one card, which is what the
         print page picks up by reference. -->
    <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value">
      <Markdown>*Nothing picked yet — browse the deck above and click **This one's me** on the card that sounds most like you.*</Markdown>
      <Vertical id="wj1_type_result">

        <!-- The choice, spelled out. The stored value is a CODE
             (`community_writer`), so a <Ref> would print machine text; these
             when= lines mount the one human sentence that matches, and they
             travel with wj1_type_result into the artifact tab and the PDF. -->
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'community_writer'">**The type I chose:** 🤝 Community Writer</Markdown>
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'explorer'"        >**The type I chose:** 🧭 Explorer</Markdown>
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'technician'"      >**The type I chose:** 🔧 Technician</Markdown>
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'hobbyist'"        >**The type I chose:** 🌿 Hobbyist</Markdown>
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'dreamer'"         >**The type I chose:** 💭 Dreamer</Markdown>
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'practitioner'"    >**The type I chose:** 🔁 Practitioner</Markdown>
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'finisher'"        >**The type I chose:** 🏁 Finisher</Markdown>
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'pragmatist'"      >**The type I chose:** 🎯 Pragmatist</Markdown>
        <Markdown when="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'custom'"          >**The type I chose:** ✍️ {{@wj1_type_custom_name.value}} *(one I named myself)*</Markdown>

        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'community_writer'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Use ref="wj1_card_community_writer_text" />
        </IntakeGate>

        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'explorer'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Use ref="wj1_card_explorer_text" />
        </IntakeGate>

        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'technician'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Use ref="wj1_card_technician_text" />
        </IntakeGate>

        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'hobbyist'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Use ref="wj1_card_hobbyist_text" />
        </IntakeGate>

        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'dreamer'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Use ref="wj1_card_dreamer_text" />
        </IntakeGate>

        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'practitioner'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Use ref="wj1_card_practitioner_text" />
        </IntakeGate>

        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'finisher'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Use ref="wj1_card_finisher_text" />
        </IntakeGate>

        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'pragmatist'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Use ref="wj1_card_pragmatist_text" />
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- The written-your-own card, rebuilt from the student's two inputs
             rather than <Use>d: the authored block holds the empty controls, and
             what belongs in the journal is what they typed. Deliberately NOT a
             table — a multi-line TextArea inside a markdown table cell collapses
             the table. -->
        <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_type_choice.value === 'custom'">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wj1_card_custom_result">
**✍️ {{@wj1_type_custom_name.value}}** — a writer type I named myself.

{{@wj1_type_custom_text.value}}
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

      </Vertical>
    </IntakeGate>

    <Markdown>
Once you've chosen, think about what makes you sure — and about the parts of the card that don't quite
land — and answer the question below.
    </Markdown>

    <!-- <Use>, not <Ref>: a Ref renders the STORED value, which is now a code
         like `figure_out`. <Use> re-renders the dropdown itself, so the student
         reads their own sentence back (and can nudge it, as on the board). -->
    <Collapsible id="wj1_type_lookback" title="Remind me what I said about why I write">
      <Markdown>**💬 Messaging &amp; staying in touch:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_messaging" />
      <Markdown>**🎓 Coursework &amp; class writing:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_coursework" />
      <Markdown>**🗂️ Notes, lists &amp; organizing:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_organizing" />
      <Markdown>**📣 Posting &amp; sharing publicly:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_posting" />
      <Markdown>**🎨 Creative &amp; personal writing:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_creative" />
    </Collapsible>

    <!-- The old "Does this feel like you?" Yes/Partly/Not really question
         (wj1_fit) was removed with the Aug 2026 revision: once the student picks
         the card themselves, asking whether it feels like them answers itself.
         The story below carries the whole weight now, and it is a better prompt
         for it — defending a choice is writing; rating a machine's guess isn't. -->

    <Markdown>
### Your reflection

You picked that card. Now show your work: **tell a story from your writing life that shows why this type
fits you — or where it doesn't quite.**

A real moment, not a summary. What you were writing, what happened, what it took. If the card gets you
mostly right but misses something, the story is the place to say so — the part it can't hold is often the
most interesting thing about a writer.
    </Markdown>
    <TextArea id="wj1_profile_reflect" rows="9" placeholder="Here's a story from my writing life..." />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       PRINT &amp; SUBMIT  —  THE SUBMISSION PAGE
       The one page that gets printed. It is mounted twice, and only ever one of
       those mounts is visible: as the "Print &amp; Submit" tab of
       writing_journal1_sba_part1.olx, and as the chat's final right-pane screen
       (`wj1_sidebar <- wj1_print`). Tabs keep every panel mounted but hide the
       inactive ones with display:none, which print inherits, so the PDF carries
       exactly one copy.

       Everything here must be MOUNTED at print time: window.print() photographs
       the live DOM, and a block React never rendered cannot be recovered by a
       print stylesheet. Three consequences, all load-bearing — do not "tidy"
       them away:
         * NO <Collapsible>. It renders children only when expanded, so a
           collapsed section exports as a bare heading.
         * NO <Tabs>, NO <UseHistory>. Both show one child at a time.
         * <IntakeGate> and when= ARE fine, and are used deliberately: they
           mount the branch the student earned, so the writer-type card prints
           as theirs alone.

       ORDER IS AN INVERTED TRIANGLE — what an instructor most needs to read
       comes first: the controls (print-hidden), then everything the student
       WROTE, then the structured answers, so the page can be scanned in a
       couple of minutes without paging.

       <Use> is a DAG reference, not a copy — the grids and the type card here
       are the SAME nodes as on the working screens, so they always agree.
       =================================================================== -->

  <!-- The chat's final screen: a light wrap-up that points at the Print &
       Submit tab. The full print page lives on the tab only; the button here
       is the manual path if the student doesn't go on their own. -->
  <Vertical id="wj1_wrapup">
    <Markdown>
## That's the Journal

Your whole journal — the board, your reasons, the writer type you chose, and your reflection — is gathered on the
**Print &amp; Submit** tab up top. Give it a read. When you're happy with it, click the confirm box there and
export it as a PDF for your course system.
    </Markdown>
    <ActionButton id="wj1_open_print_btn" label="Open Print &amp; Submit">
      <!-- activeTab is positional: 3 = Print &amp; Submit in wj1_sba_tabs. -->
      <SetFieldAction id="wj1_open_print_set" target="wj1_sba_tabs" field="activeTab" value="3" />
      <ScrollToAction id="wj1_open_print_scroll" target="wj1_print" />
    </ActionButton>
  </Vertical>

  <Vertical id="wj1_print">

    <!-- The controls: on screen only. print="false" -> class print-hide, so the
         heading, the confirm box, the button and the how-to never reach the PDF. -->
    <Vertical id="wj1_print_controls" print="false">

      <Markdown>
## Print &amp; Submit

Here is your whole journal on one page, in the order your instructor will read it — your writing first.
Read it over; if you want to change an answer, go back to that step in the conversation, edit it, and come
back. Everything below updates to match.

When it looks right, click the box and the **Export as PDF** button will appear. Save the file, then upload
it to your course system.
      </Markdown>

      <CheckboxInput id="wj1_completion_page">
        <Markdown>**Confirm that you are done with this journal entry.**</Markdown>
        <Key id="wj1_finished" value="wj1_finished">I don't need to make any more changes.</Key>
      </CheckboxInput>
      <!-- Ticking the confirm box brings the student to the print tab (once;
           activeTab is positional: 3 = Print &amp; Submit in wj1_sba_tabs). -->
      <Trigger id="wj1_finished_jump" watch="'wj1_finished' in @wj1_completion_page.value" mode="once">
        <SetFieldAction id="wj1_finished_jump_set" target="wj1_sba_tabs" field="activeTab" value="3" />
        <ScrollToAction id="wj1_finished_jump_scroll" target="wj1_print" />
      </Trigger>

      <!-- Gated on the confirm box, matching WJ2. ActionButtons are hidden from
           print by print.css anyway; the wrapper above hides the rest. -->
      <ActionButton id="wj1_print_btn" label="Export as PDF"
                    when="'wj1_finished' in @wj1_completion_page.value">
        <PrintAction id="wj1_print_action" />
      </ActionButton>

      <Markdown when="'wj1_finished' in @wj1_completion_page.value">
## Journal Complete

In this journal you did the thing the assignment was really asking for: you stopped and **noticed the
writing you already do.** You built a **Writer Board** — for school and outside it — put the **why** on
it, read the Circle's eight **writer types** and **chose** the one that sounds like you, and then did the
most important part: you told the story that shows why.

Hold onto Alma's point: a writer type is a **snapshot, not a verdict.** The writing you're about to do in
this course will keep changing the picture — which is exactly why it was worth mapping where you're
starting from.
      </Markdown>

    </Vertical>

    <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------------
         1. THE STUDENT'S WRITING  (what the instructor actually reads)
         Longest and most considered first: the story, then the two shorter
         reflections, then the open boxes that top up the structured items.
